  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Package Scripts**\n\nOkay, I\'m digging into the `package.json` scripts now. Currently, I\'m reviewing the existing setup. I see scripts for development, build, preview, cleaning, and linting. Nothing strikes me as particularly unusual.\n\n\n**Updating Script Adjustments**\n\nI\'ve just gotten instructions on how to adjust the \"dev\", \"build\", and \"start\" scripts if I modify `server.ts`. I also need to be sure to restart the dev server after making these changes. Now, I will start to consider Staff screen requirements, focusing on the simple interface design with STAFF001 and a single \"PUSH\" button. The screen needs to be very straightforward, no extras, and the button changes to \"PUSHED\" and becomes disabled after being pressed.\n\n\n**Implementing Staff Login**\n\nI am now implementing the staff login page. It\'s clean and professional, with dummy credentials clearly labeled. The design is simple, and upon successful login, the staff member is immediately directed to the staff screen. I\'ve also added a check to handle duplicate pushes; the backend will reject them with an appropriate message. I\'m getting ready to implement `src/geofence.ts` next.\n\n\n**Defining Geofencing Logic**\n\nI am now focusing on the geofencing logic. I am planning the implementation of `server.ts` to manage API endpoints. This includes login, status checks, and the crucial `/api/push` endpoint. I\'ll make sure to handle duplicate pushes and validate the latitude and longitude, as well as add a timestamp. The core of this function will be saving the pushed data to both an Excel file and a JSON file.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 4
      nanos: 807925453
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
